INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (859,1,'2026-02-08 18:01:29','2026-02-08 18:01:29','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>02/11/2020</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img src=\"https://nucktrajkoprokopiev.mk/wp-content/uploads/2020/11/PLAKAT-TP-color-02.jpg\" alt=\"\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Trajko Prokopiev (6.11.1909 – 20.01.1978) was a great Macedonian composer, conductor, music educator, and organizer of musical life in the Republic of Macedonia. One of the most consistent advocates in building the Macedonian national musical direction, he was a composer who painted the human soul — deeply thoughtful and sensitive, with a broad Slavic spirit — leaving behind scores of lasting value and works with humane messages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Born in Kumanovo in 1909, he graduated from the Music School in Belgrade in 1934. He became the first director of the Secondary Music School in Skopje in 1944, studied at the Prague Conservatory in 1946–47, served as head of the music program at Radio Skopje and conductor of the Radio Symphony Orchestra. In 1948 he worked as conductor of the “Kocho Racin” choir and received an award from the Ministry of Education and Culture of the People’s Republic of Macedonia for the choral piece “Lenka.” In 1949 he debuted as an opera conductor with the premiere of Smetana’s <em>The Bartered Bride</em>. From 1951–54 he conducted Verdi’s <em>Il Trovatore</em>, symphonic concerts of the Macedonian Philharmonic, and Verdi’s <em>Rigoletto</em>, as well as the premieres of the ballet <em>Coppélia</em> by Léo Delibes and the opera <em>The Barber of Seville</em> by Gioachino Rossini. In 1963 he returned as conductor at the Macedonian National Theatre. With a group of Macedonian composers he visited Georgia and met Dmitri Shostakovich in Leningrad. He also served as director of the Macedonian Folk Song and Dance Ensemble “Tanec.” In 1967 he received the October Lifetime Achievement Award of SR Macedonia. In 1969, with the “Kocho Racin” choir, he won first prize at the VIII International Choral Festival in Gorizia, Italy. He retired in 1975, received the AVNOJ Award for Music in 1977, completed the orchestration of the opera <em>Kuzman Kapidan</em> in 1978, and died in 1979 in Belgrade. He is buried in Skopje, in the Alley of the Greats at the Butel City Cemetery.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>His name represents one of the most prolific and significant in Macedonian composition, with five decades of creative work forming part of the history of Macedonian musical culture. His valuable works include choral pieces such as “Lenka,” “Kumanovki I–VI,” “Ilindenka,” “Ochi,” “Svadbarska,” “Tugjina,” “Rosa,” “Rakatka II,” and others, as well as sacred music, numerous chamber compositions, stage and film music (<em>Chorbaji Teodos</em>, <em>Begalka</em>, <em>Ilinden 1903</em>, <em>Komitska Legenda</em>), and major stage works — the ballet <em>Labin and Dojrana</em> and the operas <em>Razdelba</em> and <em>Kuzman Kapidan</em> — all part of the anthology of Macedonian music, woven with themes, expressions, and rhythmic structures drawn from Macedonian folklore. These works radiate the beauty and spirit of the Macedonian land.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>“…To this grave all of Macedonia will come, where true lovers and connoisseurs of musical art will pay their respects… From the father of Macedonian musical culture will remain his creative message, his compositional thought as a bright, shining example and guide…”<br>— Vlastimir Nikolovski,<br>on behalf of the Society of Composers of Macedonia.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','AN EVENING DEDICATED TO TRAJKO PROKOPIEV — 06.11.2020 (FRIDAY) — THEATRE HALL — 19:00','','publish','open','open','','an-evening-dedicated-to-trajko-prokopiev-06-11-2020-friday-theatre-hall-1900','','','2026-02-08 18:01:29','2026-02-08 18:01:29','',0,'https://user17.delovna.finki.ukim.mk/?p=859',0,'post','',0);
INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (860,1,'2026-02-08 18:01:29','2026-02-08 18:01:29','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>02/11/2020</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img src=\"https://nucktrajkoprokopiev.mk/wp-content/uploads/2020/11/PLAKAT-TP-color-02.jpg\" alt=\"\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Trajko Prokopiev (6.11.1909 – 20.01.1978) was a great Macedonian composer, conductor, music educator, and organizer of musical life in the Republic of Macedonia. One of the most consistent advocates in building the Macedonian national musical direction, he was a composer who painted the human soul — deeply thoughtful and sensitive, with a broad Slavic spirit — leaving behind scores of lasting value and works with humane messages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Born in Kumanovo in 1909, he graduated from the Music School in Belgrade in 1934. He became the first director of the Secondary Music School in Skopje in 1944, studied at the Prague Conservatory in 1946–47, served as head of the music program at Radio Skopje and conductor of the Radio Symphony Orchestra. In 1948 he worked as conductor of the “Kocho Racin” choir and received an award from the Ministry of Education and Culture of the People’s Republic of Macedonia for the choral piece “Lenka.” In 1949 he debuted as an opera conductor with the premiere of Smetana’s <em>The Bartered Bride</em>. From 1951–54 he conducted Verdi’s <em>Il Trovatore</em>, symphonic concerts of the Macedonian Philharmonic, and Verdi’s <em>Rigoletto</em>, as well as the premieres of the ballet <em>Coppélia</em> by Léo Delibes and the opera <em>The Barber of Seville</em> by Gioachino Rossini. In 1963 he returned as conductor at the Macedonian National Theatre. With a group of Macedonian composers he visited Georgia and met Dmitri Shostakovich in Leningrad. He also served as director of the Macedonian Folk Song and Dance Ensemble “Tanec.” In 1967 he receiv